{"id":2007,"date":"2017-03-23T11:54:09","date_gmt":"2017-03-23T18:54:09","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/?p=2007"},"modified":"2018-08-15T01:49:33","modified_gmt":"2018-08-15T01:49:33","slug":"the-international-cloud-atlas-has-been-added-as-a-link","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/2017\/03\/23\/the-international-cloud-atlas-has-been-added-as-a-link\/","title":{"rendered":"The International Cloud Atlas has been added as a Link"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Welcome to the official site of the World Meteorological Organization\u2019s (WMO) International Cloud Atlas. This Atlas describes the classification system for clouds and meteorological phenomena used by all WMO Members. The classifications also describe meteorological meteors other than clouds \u2013 hydrometeors, lithometeors, photometeors, and electrometeors.<\/p>\n<p>The Atlas provides a common language to communicate cloud observations, and ensures consistency in reporting by observers around the world. It serves as a training tool for meteorologists, as well as for those working in aeronautical and maritime environments, and it has become popular with weather enthusiasts and cloud spotters.<\/p>\n<div id=\"attachment_345277\" style=\"width: 610px\" class=\"wp-caption alignnone\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-345277\" data-attachment-id=\"345277\" data-permalink=\"http:\/\/ccra.us\/2017\/03\/23\/the-international-cloud-atlas-has-been-added-as-a-link\/a_pw3dna\/\" data-orig-file=\"\/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?fit=5184%2C3888\" data-orig-size=\"5184,3888\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"A_Pw3Dna\" data-image-description=\"&lt;p&gt;\u00a9 Albert de Nijs&lt;br \/&gt;\nLima NV, Sweden&lt;br \/&gt;\nLatitude: 60\u00b0 56&#8242; 4&#8221; N&lt;br \/&gt;\nLongitude: 13\u00b0 21&#8242; 44&#8221; E&lt;br \/&gt;\n22 December 2014 1634 (Local Time)&lt;br \/&gt;\nCamera direction: towards W&lt;br \/&gt;\nImage I.D.: P.10U.1&lt;br \/&gt;\nPolar Stratospheric clouds : Nacreous&lt;br \/&gt;\nPolar stratospheric clouds composed primarily of ice are known as nacreous, or mother-of-pearl clouds. After sunset they are characterised by brilliant iridescent colours as shown in this photograph from Sweden.&lt;br \/&gt;\nNacreous clouds only form in the stratosphere at a temperature below the ice frost point, typically near -85\u00b0C, in the high latitudes of both hemispheres during winter. They are often lenticular in shape.&lt;br \/&gt;\n&#8211; See more at: https:\/\/www.wmocloudatlas.org\/upper-atmospheric-clouds.html#sthash.GUc2O8N7.dpuf&lt;\/p&gt;\n\" data-image-caption=\"&lt;p&gt;\u00a9 Albert de Nijs&lt;br \/&gt;\nLima NV, Sweden&lt;br \/&gt;\nLatitude: 60\u00b0 56&#8242; 4&#8221; N&lt;br \/&gt;\nLongitude: 13\u00b0 21&#8242; 44&#8221; E&lt;br \/&gt;\n22 December 2014 1634 (Local Time)&lt;br \/&gt;\nCamera direction: towards W&lt;br \/&gt;\nImage I.D.: P.10U.1&lt;br \/&gt;\nPolar Stratospheric clouds : Nacreous&lt;br \/&gt;\nPolar stratospheric clouds composed primarily of ice are known as nacreous, or mother-of-pearl clouds. After sunset they are characterised by brilliant iridescent colours as shown in this photograph from Sweden.&lt;br \/&gt;\nNacreous clouds only form in the stratosphere at a temperature below the ice frost point, typically near -85\u00b0C, in the high latitudes of both hemispheres during winter. They are often lenticular in shape.&lt;br \/&gt;\n&#8211; See more at: https:\/\/www.wmocloudatlas.org\/upper-atmospheric-clouds.html#sthash.GUc2O8N7.dpuf&lt;\/p&gt;\n\" data-medium-file=\"\/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?fit=300%2C225\" data-large-file=\"\/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?fit=600%2C450\" class=\"size-large wp-image-345277\" src=\"\/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?resize=600%2C450\" alt=\"\u00a9 Albert de Nijs&lt;br \/&gt; Lima NV, Sweden&lt;br \/&gt; Latitude: 60\u00b0 56' 4'' N&lt;br \/&gt; Longitude: 13\u00b0 21' 44'' E&lt;br \/&gt; 22 December 2014 1634 (Local Time)&lt;br \/&gt; Camera direction: towards W&lt;br \/&gt; Image I.D.: P.10U.1&lt;br \/&gt; Polar Stratospheric clouds : Nacreous&lt;br \/&gt; Polar stratospheric clouds composed primarily of ice are known as nacreous, or mother-of-pearl clouds. After sunset they are characterised by brilliant iridescent colours as shown in this photograph from Sweden.&lt;br \/&gt; Nacreous clouds only form in the stratosphere at a temperature below the ice frost point, typically near -85\u00b0C, in the high latitudes of both hemispheres during winter. They are often lenticular in shape.&lt;br \/&gt; - See more at: https:\/\/www.wmocloudatlas.org\/upper-atmospheric-clouds.html#sthash.GUc2O8N7.dpuf\" width=\"600\" height=\"450\" srcset=\"\/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?resize=1024%2C768 1024w, \/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?resize=300%2C225 300w, \/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?resize=768%2C576 768w, \/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?resize=350%2C263 350w, \/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?w=1200 1200w, \/archive\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/08\/A_Pw3Dna.jpg?w=1800 1800w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px\" \/><p id=\"caption-attachment-345277\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">\u00a9 Albert de Nijs<\/p>\n<p>Lima NV, Sweden<\/p>\n<p>Latitude: 60\u00b0 56&#8242; 4&#8221; N<\/p>\n<p>Longitude: 13\u00b0 21&#8242; 44&#8221; E<\/p>\n<p>22 December 2014 1634 (Local Time)<\/p>\n<p>Camera direction: towards W<\/p>\n<p>Image I.D.: P.10U.1<\/p>\n<p>Polar Stratospheric clouds : Nacreous<\/p>\n<p>Polar stratospheric clouds composed primarily of ice are known as nacreous, or mother-of-pearl clouds. After sunset they are characterised by brilliant iridescent colours as shown in this photograph from Sweden.<\/p>\n<p>Nacreous clouds only form in the stratosphere at a temperature below the ice frost point, typically near -85\u00b0C, in the high latitudes of both hemispheres during winter. They are often lenticular in shape.<\/p>\n<p>&#8211; See more at: https:\/\/www.wmocloudatlas.org\/upper-atmospheric-clouds.html#sthash.GUc2O8N7.dpuf<\/p><\/div>\n<p>For more information please use the following link to the <a title=\"International Cloud Atlas\" href=\"https:\/\/www.wmocloudatlas.org\/home.html\">International Cloud Atlas<\/a>.<\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Welcome to the official site of the World Meteorological Organization\u2019s (WMO) International Cloud Atlas. This Atlas describes the classification system for clouds and meteorological phenomena used by all WMO Members. <a class=\"more-link\" href=\"http:\/\/ccra.us\/2017\/03\/23\/the-international-cloud-atlas-has-been-added-as-a-link\/\">Continue Reading &rarr;<\/a><\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"_jetpack_newsletter_access":"","_jetpack_dont_email_post_to_subs":false,"_jetpack_newsletter_tier_id":0,"_jetpack_memberships_contains_paywalled_content":false,"_jetpack_memberships_contains_paid_content":false,"footnotes":""},"categories":[2],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-2007","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-ccra-news"],"jetpack_featured_media_url":"","jetpack_sharing_enabled":true,"jetpack_shortlink":"https:\/\/wp.me\/p69ory-wn","_links":{"self":[{"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/2007","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/1"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=2007"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/2007\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":345292,"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/2007\/revisions\/345292"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=2007"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"category","embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/categories?post=2007"},{"taxonomy":"post_tag","embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/ccra.us\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/tags?post=2007"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}